  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nWednesday morning, upper ridging move off east into the Great Lakes \nregion, along with a upper trough trailing behind, moving into the \nnorthern Plains. Mid level cyclogenesis starts to take shape over \nthe Dakotas Wednesday morning, intensifying throughout the day. \nWhile high pressure settling to the surface from the outgoing ridge \ncould bring highs into the low to mid 60s across southwest to \neastern portions of our CWA, an incoming cold front from the low \npressure system will keep highs in the low to mid 50s across \nportions of north central Nebraska and the Eastern Panhandle. \n\nThis front will also bring about the mention of elevated to near \ncritical fire weather concerns across the western portions of our \nCWA. Strong westerly winds at 15 to 25 mph will be possible, with \ngusts up to 50 mph not out of the question for the western Sandhills \nand the Pine Ridge area. Dry air from the high pressure aloft along \nwith a dry air mass moving in with the front will create humidity \nvalues dropping into the low 20s across southwest to north central \nNebraska. While conditions are forecasted to remain just below Red \nFlag criteria, this will need to be monitored for if conditions \ncontinue to trend in that direction. \n\nAs troughing moves off northeast into Canada and weakens, ridging \nwill again build back into the region and remain through Friday \nafternoon. Daytime highs Thursday will be in the the low to mid 50s \nbehind the cold front, keeping highs near climatological norms.\nThis will be brief however as temperatures will bounce back to \nthe upper 50s to mid 60s across southwest to central Nebraska, \npushing 8 to 12 degrees above normal. \n\nThe next opportunity for active weather comes late Friday heading \ninto the weekend. Strong troughing from the Pacific will make \nlandfall and move across the Rockies by Friday evening. Model \noutputs have backed off on the closed low solution, favoring more of \nan open wave solution. It is important to note consistency between \nmodel runs remains to be seen to pull out confident details on the \nexact track as well as intensity of the system moving into our \nregion this weekend. However, there is growing confidence that \nwestern Nebraska has a decent probability (50 - 70 percent) of \nseeing scattered to widespread precipitation through the weekend. At \nthis time, weekend highs will be in the 40s, favoring rainy \nprecipitation. A cold front moving in Saturday morning could even \ninitiate isolated thunderstorm activity in a low CAPE, high shear \nenvironment. With highs in the upper 30s across north central \nNebraska and cooling overnight lows, this could bring a rain/snow \nmix to even briefly all snow and leaving accumulations on the \nground. This system still remains offshore currently and with \ntrajectories changing every model run, should be monitored \nclosely if future forecast packages can nail down the timing and\nintensity of this system.",
